# Screenglaze: read access to the two physical buttons, and to nothing else.
#
# TAG+="uaccess" rather than the 'input' group, on purpose. Putting the user in
# 'input' hands EVERY program they run the whole touchscreen -- that is a
# keylogger for anything typed on the on-screen keyboard, granted permanently,
# to get at two buttons. 'uaccess' grants an ACL to the user of the currently
# active seat session only, and only on the devices named here.
#
# The number has to be LOWER than 73: 73-seat-late.rules is what turns the tag
# into an actual ACL, and a tag set after it runs is a tag nobody reads.
#
# The names are this phone's (POCO X3 NFC, sm7150). If a kernel update renames
# them the combo stops working silently -- `screenglaze --check` is what
# says so, and `cat /proc/bus/input/devices` is where the new names are.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ATTRS{name}=="pm8941_pwrkey", TAG+="uaccess"
SUBSYSTEM=="input", KERNEL=="event*", ATTRS{name}=="pm8941_resin", TAG+="uaccess"

# And /dev/uinput, to hand the volume key BACK.
#
# Screenglaze takes volume-down away from the compositor for 150 ms to see
# whether a screenshot is starting. If it is not, the press has to be replayed,
# and a virtual input device is the only way to put a key event back into the
# system. Without this the key would simply be eaten.
#
# static_node because /dev/uinput belongs to a module and may exist before any
# uevent that udev could react to; the tag alone would then apply to nothing.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput", TAG+="uaccess"
